  This research introduces ReaNN, a novel artificial neural network model that can dynamically adjust data rates in response to changing network conditions, thereby minimizing energy consumption while ensuring seamless performance of ANNs.  Developed using a lightweight state machine and a one-dimensional convolutional neural network, ReaNN can adapt to various network scenarios and securely prevent malicious data transmissions.

  The proposed hardware accelerator, a reconfigurable analog neural network (RAN), can dynamically adjust data rates in response to changing network conditions, allowing for a significant reduction in energy consumption of up to 13.68% in optimal scenarios and a remarkable 6.89% in a more conservative estimation, a 23.12% increase in accelerator lifespan, and a 15% decrease in a typical cost incurred by a device in a real-world application.  A design example is presented in a 180nm process technology with a 64-bit wide SPI interface and a sample I2S application are provided to demonstrate the versatility of RAN.  A case study highlighting the atherosclerosis-like damage that a device suffers from when using fixed data rates is also included to emphasize the benefits of dynamic data rate adjustment.  A prototype of RAN is implemented and shows that, compared to a static design, it has a 2.5 times larger lifetime at a certain performance level and a 35% decrease in a typical cost incurred by a device in a real-world application.  A comparative analysis with existing works is conducted to demonstrate the uniqueness of RAN.  A discussion on the potential risks of using dynamic data rates and the necessity for a more secure verification framework for neural networks is also initiated.  A design example, a case study, implementation results, comparative analysis, and future work are presented in the paper.  A prototype of ReaNN and the atherosclerosis-like damage that a device suffers from when using fixed data rates are shown in Figure 9 and Figure 10, respectively, in the paper.  The results are validated through a simulation, as shown in Table 4, in the paper.  The proposed ReaNN model is valuable for improving the energy efficiency, lifespan, and performance of ANNs in communication systems.
